    "problem_statement": "### Cache Timeout Issue in Tweepy\n\n#### Description\n\nIt seems there's an unexpected behavior with the cache timeout in the latest update. The cache entries are not expiring as expected, which might be due to an alteration in the timeout calculation.\n\n#### Steps to Reproduce\n\n1. Create a directory for cache testing:\n   ```python\n   import os\n   os.mkdir('cache_test_dir')\n   ```\n\n2. Initialize a `FileCache` with a specific timeout:\n   ```python\n   from tweepy.cache import FileCache\n   cache = FileCache('cache_test_dir', timeout=5)\n   ```\n\n3. Store an entry and attempt to retrieve it after the expected timeout period.\n\n4. Observe that the entry is still present beyond the expected expiration time.\n\n5. Clean up the test directory:\n   ```python\n   import shutil\n   shutil.rmtree('cache_test_dir')\n   ```\n\n#### Expected Behavior\n\nCache entries should expire and be removed after the specified timeout period.\n\n#### Environment\n\n- Tweepy version: [Specify version]\n- Python version: 3.10.16\n- OS: [Specify OS]\n\n#### Additional Information\n\nThis issue affects both `FileCache` and `MemoryCache` implementations. It would be great to have a look into the timeout logic to ensure it aligns with the intended behavior."
